  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> A biologist receives bird population estimates for different parks. The estimates are shown in the table. Work
    with your partner to arrange these estimates in order from least to greatest. </item>

  <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>Park</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Bird Population</emphasis></entry></row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Shady Elm
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(7.5\times10^3\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Westcott Park
        </entry>
        <entry>
          105,000
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Brighton Park
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(4.5\times10^2\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Red Elk
        </entry>
        <entry>
        22,300
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
        Bear Park
        </entry>
        <entry>
          \(1.1\times10^5\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
         Daniels Park
        </entry>
        <entry>
          3,300
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
         Blue Spruce
        </entry>
        <entry>
        \(1.25\times10^4\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          Yellow Jay Park
        </entry>
        <entry>
      \(2.25\times10^4\)
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  
  <para>Answer:</para>
</list>
<list>
  <item>Brighton Park, \(4.5\times10^2\)</item>
  <item>Daniels Park, 3,300</item>
  <item>Shady Elm, \(7.5\times10^3\)</item>
  <item>Blue Spruce, \(1.25\times10^4\)</item>
  <item>Red Elk, 22,300</item>
  <item>Yellow Jay Park, \(2.25\times10^4\)</item>
  <item>Westcott Park, 105,000</item>
  <item>Bear Park, \(1.1\times10^5\)</item>
</list>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">

  <item> Enrico weighs the small objects shown for a science project. Work with your partner to arrange these weights in
    order from heaviest to lightest. </item>
</list>

<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Object</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Weight (lb)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
      grain of sand
      </entry>
      <entry>
       \(1.4\times10^{-4}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       grain of pollen
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0.00001014
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
      one drop of water
      </entry>
      <entry>
    0.00011
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        a feather
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(1.8\times10^{-5}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        one kernel of corn
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(1.1\times10^{-3}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        house fly
      </entry>
      <entry>
       \(3.08\times10^{-5}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Answer:</para>
<list>
  <item>one kernel of corn, \(1.1\times10^{-3}\) </item>
  <item>grain of sand, \(1.4\times10^{-4}\) </item>
  <item>one drop of water, 0.00011 </item>
  <item>house fly, \(3.08\times10^{-5}\) </item>
  <item>a feather, \(1.8\times10^{-5}\) </item>
  <item>grain of pollen, 0.00001014 </item>
</list>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> What do you notice when comparing numbers in scientific notation with different negative exponents?
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> If both exponents are negative, then the number with the smaller absolute value exponent is
    the larger number. For example, the number with the exponent –3 is larger than the number with the exponent
    –4.
  </item>
</list>
<title>5.6.5: Self Check</title>
<para><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts
      explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
        <para>QUESTION</para>
        <para>Which of the numbers shown is the greatest?</para>
        <table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
      <entry>
       0.0065
      </entry>
      <entry>
       Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Compare the numbers in the same form. 0.0065 is equivalent
          to \(6.5\times10^{-3}\). This is not the greatest number. The answer is \(7.2\times10^{-3}\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(7.2\times10^{-3}\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: The number \(7.2\times10^{-3}\) is the largest value. It is equivalent
          to 0.0072.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       0.00084
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Compare the numbers in the same form. 0.00084 is equivalent
          to \(8.4\times10^{-4}\). \(10^{-4}\) is less than \(10^{-3}\). The answer is \(7.2\times10^{-3}\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       \(9.6\times10^{-5}\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: \(10^{-5}\) is less than \(10^{-4}\). \(9.6\times10^{-5}\)
          is equivalent to 0.000096. The answer is \(7.2\times10^{-3}\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>5.6.5: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with
      the self check. Students will not automatically have access to the content, so you may wish to share it with those
      who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Applying Scientific Notation</title>
<para>Let’s look at how to solve a real-world problem involving scientific notation.</para>
<para>The specimens below are measured lengths of different tiny animals. Arrange them in order from shortest to longest.
</para>

<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Specimen</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Length (m)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        A
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(7.0\times10^{-3}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        B
      </entry>
      <entry>
      0.0000847
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       C
      </entry>
      <entry>
       0.00022
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       D
      </entry>
      <entry>
     \(1.8\times10^{-4}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       E
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(8.92\times10^{-3}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        F
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(6.03\times10^{-2}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>To solve this problem, we can convert all of the measurements to decimal form or standard form.</para>
<para>Let’s convert all measurements to decimal form from scientific notation.</para>

<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Specimen</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Length (m)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
       A
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(7.0\times10^{-3} = 0.007\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       B
      </entry>
      <entry>
         0.0000847
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
     C
      </entry>
      <entry>
         0.00022
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
      D
      </entry>
      <entry>
         \(1.8\times10^{-4} = 0.00018\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
      E
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(8.92\times10^{-3} = 0.00892\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       F
      </entry>
      <entry>
     \(6.03\times10^{-2} = 0.0603\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>We can now arrange the decimal values in order from shortest to longest:</para>
<para>B, D, C, A, E, F</para>
<para>Let’s convert each length into scientific notation and check our answer.</para>

<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Specimen</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Length (m)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        A
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(7.0\times10^{-3}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       B
      </entry>
      <entry>
      \(0.0000847 = 8.47\times 10^{-5}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       C
      </entry>
      <entry>
       \(0.00022 = 2.2 \times 10^{-4}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       D
      </entry>
      <entry>
       \(1.8\times10^{-4}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        E
      </entry>
      <entry>
       \(8.92\times10^{-3}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        F
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(6.03\times10^{-2}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>The negative exponent with the largest absolute value represents the smallest number, so B is the shortest length.
</para>
<para>The negative exponent with the smallest absolute value represents the largest number, so F is the longest length.</para>
<para>When the exponents are equal, compare the decimal part of the number.</para>
<para>The order B, D, C, A, E, F is correct from shortest to longest length.</para>
<title>Try It: Applying Scientific Notation</title>
<para>The measurements in the table are sample wavelengths for the given types of waves. Arrange them in order from
  shortest to longest.</para>

<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Color</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Wavelength (m)</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        red light
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(7.0\times10^{-7}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        blue light
      </entry>
      <entry>
       0.00000045
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       infrared light
      </entry>
      <entry>
      \(5.0\times10^{-4}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       ultraviolet light
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(2.0\times10^{-9}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
       microwaves
      </entry>
      <entry>
       \(1.5\times10^{-1}\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        radio waves
      </entry>
      <entry>
       2.1
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to arrange the measurements from shortest to longest:</para>
<para>Let’s convert the values for blue light and radio waves into scientific notation to compare all the values.</para>
<para>blue light = \(0.00000045 = 4.5\times 10^{-7}\)</para>
<para>radio waves = \(2.1 = 2.1\times 10^0\)</para>
<para>So, from shortest to longest the wavelengths are:</para>
<para>ultraviolet light, \(2.0\times10^{-9}\)
  blue light, \(4.5\times10^{-7}\) 
  red light, \(7.0\times10^{-7}\)
  infrared light, \(5.0\times10^{-4}\) microwaves, \(1.5\times10^{-1}\) 
  radio waves, \(2.1\times10^0\) </para></section>
</content>
